Global changeClimate change boosts Salmonella antimicrobial resistance
                    Agustina Taglialegna1

Nature Reviews Microbiology

(2026) Cite this article









            Save article




                View saved research

Subjects

            Antimicrobial resistanceClimate-change impacts

Access through your institution







                        Buy or subscribe

Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) is a major public health concern globally. Among the many antimicrobial-resistant pathogens, drug-resistant Salmonella species are of particular concern, as they are among the leading causes of gastrointestinal infections in humans and animals worldwide and listed as a high-priority pathogen by the World Health Organization. Climate change can contribute to the emergence and spread of infections caused by antimicrobial-resistant pathogens, but quantitative data linking climate factors and antimicrobial resistance genes (ARGs) in pathogens such as Salmonella are lacking. In a recent study, Zhou, Teng, Geng and colleagues analysed genome data of ~480,000 Salmonella strains from 139 countries and regions, between 1940 and 2023. As indicators of climate change, they used mean temperatures and annual precipitation levels. The authors integrated these data using the Tobit model and generalized additive models and quantified climate-driven changes in ARG abundance. Results showed that over that time period, increasing ARG abundance in Salmonella was correlated with warming and changes in precipitation. Projection of future ARG trends to 2100 revealed that warming and shifts in precipitation patterns are likely to exacerbate AMR risk under high-emission scenarios. However, achieving low-emission targets and strengthening antibiotic stewardship programmes could substantially reduce the burden of Salmonella ARGs.



